<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmo Samaj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Brahmo Sabha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brahmo Samaj is the society of Brahmoism, a monotheistic reform movement begun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1828 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Raja Ram Mohan Roy</a> and Dwarkanath Tagore during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Its members denied that any scripture had authority above reason and conscience, denounced polytheism and idol-worship and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system. The Samaj condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>, discouraged child marriage and polygamy and campaigned for widow remarriage. After Roy's death in 1833 Debendranath Tagore and his circle took it up in 1843; followers of Keshub Chandra Sen broke away in 1866 as the Brahmo Samaj of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the Sadharan Brahmo Samaj was founded in 1878.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Brahmo Samaj means the community of those who have knowledge of Brahman, the ultimate reality. The Brahmo Sabha held its first assembly on 20 August 1828 at the house of Firingi Kamal Bose in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, open to people of every religion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, creed and gender. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Ram Mohan Roy</a> defined the worship: two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, Utsavananda Bidyabagish explained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> in Bengali, and songs composed by Roy or his friends followed.</p><p>In January 1830 a trust deed was executed, and on 23 January the first house of prayer, now the Adi Brahmo Samaj, on Chitpore Road was publicly inaugurated, with about 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and one Englishman present. Roy left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in November 1830 and died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> in 1833.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Roy's death attendance dwindled. Dwarkanath Tagore paid for the upkeep of the Samaj, and Ram Chandra Vidyabagish kept the weekly service going. On 6 October 1839 Debendranath Tagore, Dwarkanath's son, founded the Tattwabodhini Sabha, which had over 500 members within two years and counted Akshay Kumar Datta and Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar among its first members. In 1843 Debendranath and twenty others took the First Covenant and joined the Trust of the Brahmo Sabha. In 1861 the first branch was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> by Nobin Roy, and others followed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p>Disagreement between Debendranath Tagore and Keshub Chandra Sen came to a head between August 1865 and November 1866, and Keshub's followers formed the Brahmo Samaj of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A second split followed Keshub's handling of the Coochbehar marriage and his autocratic management, and on 15 May 1878 a meeting at the Town Hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> founded the Sadharan Brahmo Samaj, with Anandamohan Bose as president. The movements based in Calcutta declined after 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Members held that no scripture had ultimate authority above human reason and conscience, had no faith in avatars and rejected polytheism, idol-worship and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system. They took no fixed position on karma and rebirth. In social reform the Samaj condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>, discouraged child marriage and polygamy, campaigned for widow remarriage, and attacked casteism and untouchability. It also condemned the prejudice against crossing the sea.</p><p>After controversy over the underage marriage of Keshub Chandra Sen's daughter, the Special Marriages Act of 1872 set a minimum age of 14 for girls; Brahmo marriages were then solemnised under it, though many resented its declaration of not being a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Muslim or Christian. Roy's movement began the Bengal Renaissance and is described as one of the most influential religious movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2001 only 177 people declared themselves Brahmo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brahmo Samaj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ram Mohan Roy founded the <b>Brahmo Samaj</b> in 1828 to promote a rational, monotheistic Hinduism; Dayananda Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 to return to the Vedas; and Swami Vivekananda’s address to the World’s Parliament of Religions in Chicago in 1893 introduced Hindu philosophy to Western audiences.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century western publications held the Vedic religion to be unrelated to Hinduism, while reform movements such as the <b>Brahmo Samaj</b> and Neo-Vedanta urged a return to the Vedas.</blockquote><a href="/religion/vedic-religion">Vedic religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The same decades saw movements of religious and social reform, from the <b>Brahmo Samaj</b>, founded by Ram Mohan Roy in 1828, to the Arya Samaj of Dayananda Saraswati, founded in 1875, which called Hindus back to the Vedas.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1827 Ram Mohan Roy published a dissertation on the mantra; from 1830 members of the <b>Brahmo Samaj</b> used it in private devotion, and the First Covenant of 1843 required it for worship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li><li><blockquote>He wrote it in Sadhu Bhasha, a Sanskritised literary register of Bengali, as a hymn for the monotheistic <b>Brahmo Samaj</b>, and set it himself in the raga Alhaiya Bilaval, possibly with help from his grand-nephew Dinendranath Tagore.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li></ul></section>
